Modified for BenCode with MonoCode's backdrop blur (MonoCode's
`vendor/gpui-pre-apple`, branch `mc/migrate-monocode-to-gpui`):

- src/metal_renderer.rs: `backdrop_blur_marker(sigma)` marks a quad as
  frosted glass; before drawing it, the renderer copies what is already drawn
  behind it, blurs it with `MPSImageGaussianBlur` and writes it back inside
  the quad's rounded bounds (`blur_backdrop`). The layer stays readable
  (`framebuffer_only = false`) in every build so the drawable can be copied.
- src/shaders.metal: `backdrop_composite_vertex` / `_fragment`.

And so the glass shows: `shadow_fragment` in src/shaders.metal leaves a
drop shadow out of its element's bounds, as CSS `box-shadow` does, instead
of filling it underneath (which the blur spread dark across a frosted card).

And so a quiet app gives GPU memory back (after MonoCode's `trim`,
BenCode's `src/gpu_trim.rs` calling it):

- src/metal_atlas.rs: every atlas is registered; `clear_all` drops their
  tiles and textures (gpui-pre has no `PlatformAtlas::clear` to do it).
- src/metal_renderer.rs: `trim()` clears the atlases and has each
  renderer's next frame reset the instance buffers to their first 2MB; the
  path textures are made by the first frame that draws paths and dropped
  once none has for 2s, instead of with every drawable resize.

And so streaming text shapes cheaper (MonoCode's f8540190):
src/text_system.rs keeps the sized CoreText fonts it shapes with
(`sized_fonts`, up to 512) instead of copying a font for every run.

Everything else is the crates.io source unchanged. To move to a new
gpui-pre, copy its `gpui-pre-apple` here and carry those changes over.
